<h2>How hail damages different roof types</h2>

<p>Hail damage does not look the same on every roof. If you know what to look for, you are less likely to leave damage out of your claim.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Roof type</th><th>Common hail damage signs</th><th>Typical insurer pushback</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Composition (asphalt) shingle</td><td>Granule loss exposing the mat, soft “bruises,” fractured mats, cracked tabs, dark circular marks</td><td>“Wear and tear,” “blistering,” “manufacturing defect”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metal (homes, barns, shops)</td><td>Dented panels and trim, chipped or cracked coating, seam distortion</td><td>“Cosmetic only” (check for a cosmetic damage exclusion)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clay or concrete tile</td><td>Cracked, chipped, or broken tiles, slipped pieces, exposed underlayment</td><td>“Pre-existing cracks,” matching and availability disputes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat / low-slope (modified bitumen, TPO, built-up)</td><td>Membrane punctures, fractured surfacing, blistering, damaged drains and curbs</td><td>“Ponding” or age-related deterioration</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Composition shingles</h3>
<p>Shingle roofs take most of the hail and see most of the disputes. Granules protect the asphalt from UV breakdown, and once hail knocks them loose the shingle ages faster. Real hail damage tends to be random. You may find circular marks with exposed mat, or soft spots where the mat under the surface has fractured. Uniform granule wear across a whole slope usually points to age instead.</p>

<h3>Metal roofs</h3>
<p>Metal resists punctures but still dents, and hail can crack the factory coating, which may lead to corrosion. Some Texas policies include a cosmetic damage exclusion or endorsement for metal roofing. Read that language before you assume denting is or is not covered.</p>

<h3>Tile roofs</h3>
<p>Tile handles small hail well, but larger stones can crack or break pieces outright. One cracked tile can let water reach the underlayment. Finding matching tile can also be difficult, which affects whether a repair is realistic.</p>

<h3>Flat and low-slope roofs</h3>
<p>Commercial buildings and many home additions in McAllen use low-slope roofs. Hail damage there is often hard to see until water gets in, so a close inspection soon after the storm matters. For business properties, see our commercial property claims page.</p>

<h2>What to do in the first 72 hours after a hailstorm</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Stay off a wet or damaged roof. Hidden decking damage and slick surfaces cause injuries.</li>
<li><strong>Walk the property at ground level.</strong> Look for dented gutters, downspouts, A/C fins, mailboxes, window screens, and siding. Damage to these soft metals is strong supporting evidence that the roof was hit too.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and date everything.</strong> Take wide shots of each side of the house, then close-ups. If it is safe, photograph hailstones next to a coin or ruler.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage.</strong> Reasonable temporary repairs, such as tarping or catching leaks, are usually your responsibility under the policy. Keep receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim and get a professional inspection.</strong> Write down your claim number and the name of everyone you speak with.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Watch out for storm chasers</span><p>After big RGV hailstorms, out-of-area contractors often go door to door. Before you sign anything, check who the company is, read every page of the contract, and never sign over your insurance benefits without understanding what that means.</p></div>

