<h2>Filing your roof hail damage claim the right way</h2>

<h3>Start with your policy</h3>
<p>Before the adjuster comes out, look at your declarations page and policy. Check:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your wind/hail deductible.</strong> Many Texas policies use a separate wind/hail deductible, often a percentage of your Coverage A (dwelling) limit. A 2% deductible on a $250,000 dwelling limit is $5,000.</li>
<li><strong>Roof settlement terms.</strong> Replacement cost policies usually pay actual cash value first, then release recoverable depreciation after repairs are done. Some Texas roofs are covered only at actual cash value or under a roof payment schedule. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> explains the difference.</li>
<li><strong>Exclusions and endorsements</strong>, such as cosmetic damage exclusions for metal roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law coverage</strong>, which may help pay for code-required items during repair.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Submit a complete claim</h3>
<p>Give the insurer the date of loss, a clear description of the damage (roof, exterior, and interior), and your supporting photos. Be specific. “Hail damage to all slopes, gutters, and A/C unit; leak in the back bedroom ceiling” works better than “roof damage.”</p>

<h3>Know the insurer’s timeline</h3>
<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets claim-handling deadlines. Insurers generally have 15 days after notice to acknowledge the claim, start the investigation, and request information. They then have 15 business days after receiving everything they asked for to accept or reject the claim, and they can extend that by up to 45 days if they notify you. If the insurer accepts the claim, payment is generally due within 5 business days. After a declared weather-related catastrophe, these deadlines can be extended by 15 days. If your claim stalls, see our page on delayed insurance claims.</p>

