<h2>What roof hail estimates commonly miss</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Roof-Hail-Damage-Insurance-Claim-McAllen-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Roofing components including flashing, vents, and ridge cap reviewed during a hail damage estimate" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Small components and code-related items are easy to leave out of a first estimate.</figcaption></figure>

<p>A first estimate may be accurate, or it may leave things out. The items below are often missing or underpriced. Compare your estimate against this list:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Tear-off and disposal of existing layers</td><td>Extra layers increase labor and dumpster costs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Underlayment and drip edge</td><td>Required with a new roof covering and addressed in the adopted building code</td></tr>
<tr><td>Starter strip, hip and ridge cap</td><td>Priced separately from field shingles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Step, counter, and pipe flashing</td><td>Often cannot be reused once the shingles are removed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roof vents, turbines, and ridge vent</td><td>Soft metals and plastics are easily damaged by hail</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steep-slope and high-roof charges</td><td>Labor and safety factors on steep or two-story roofs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gutters, downspouts, screens, A/C fins</td><td>Exterior hail damage outside the roof itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interior repairs</td><td>Drywall, paint, and insulation affected by leaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Permit and code-related items</td><td>May be covered under ordinance or law coverage, depending on the policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>When items are missing, the usual fix is a supplement: a documented request to add scope or correct pricing. Our article on <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements/">hail damage claim supplements</a> covers how carriers handle them, and our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> service can compare your estimate line by line with the damage you documented.</p>

<h2>McAllen building codes and permits</h2>

<p>The City of McAllen adopted the 2024 International Code Council codes, including the 2024 International Residential Code, effective January 1, 2026. Re-roofing generally requires a building permit, so confirm the scope with the City’s Building Permits &amp; Inspections department before work starts. Having to meet code does not automatically mean the insurer must pay for every upgrade. That depends on your ordinance or law coverage and your policy terms. Our local building codes page has more detail.</p>

<h2>A realistic claim timeline</h2>

<p>No two claims move at the same pace, especially after a storm that damages thousands of roofs at once. The timeline below is a general guide, not a promise.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Typical timing</th><th>What you should be doing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Storm and immediate response</td><td>Days 1–3</td><td>Photograph damage, make temporary repairs, report the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acknowledgment and inspection</td><td>Within about 15 days of notice (longer after a declared catastrophe)</td><td>Be present at the inspection and point out every damaged area</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate and decision</td><td>Weeks 2–8</td><td>Review the estimate line by line and request missing items in writing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supplements and negotiation</td><td>Varies</td><td>Submit supporting photos, measurements, and pricing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repairs and recoverable depreciation</td><td>After repairs, within the policy’s deadline</td><td>Send the final invoice and completion photos to recover held-back depreciation</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>When a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>The insurance company’s adjuster works for the insurer. A public adjuster is licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance and represents only you, the policyholder. Read <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster for hail claims</a> for a side-by-side comparison.</p>

<p>Outside help is most often worth considering when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The estimate is far below your contractor’s bid or leaves out whole slopes or components.</li>
<li>The insurer calls the damage wear and tear, cosmetic, or pre-existing.</li>
<li>The claim includes interior water damage, several structures, or a commercial roof.</li>
<li>Your claim was denied. See <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-mcallen/">what to do if your hail claim was denied in McAllen</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> services cover inspection, documentation, estimating, and communication with your insurer. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins, and Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. A public adjuster cannot guarantee a particular result. What we can do is make sure your claim is complete and well supported.</p>
